[[File:S1E12 Grier Governor bond.jpg|thumb|[[Grier]] bonds with [[Megataur]]]]
A Titan will most easily bond with Seekers whose personality is similar to its previous Seeker, and Titans which have not been bonded with any Seekers for a long time may prove difficult to bond. Also, if the new Seeker's personality is completely opposite to the Titan's previous Seeker, it may outright refuse to bond; this effect is amplified if the old Seeker of a Titan has been bonded with them for a long time. This made Dante Vale think [[DeFoe]]'s Amulets were useless to the [[Huntik Foundation]] due to his intense personality and long-time use of his Titans, despite the use of [[Sorrowbond]]; however, [[Zhalia Moon]] was actually able to bond with his [[Gar-Ghoul]], possibly because of her personality having a dark side. {{Source|The Mission}}
[[File:S1E01 Lok flying bridge.png|thumb|[[Lok Lambert]] about to land safely due to the effects of bonding with [[Kipperin]]]]
The process of bonding with a Titan generally begins when someone touches an Amulet of that Titan; if the bond is successful, then the Amulet and the person may start emitting a glowing aura. Sometimes, just the process of bonding with a Titan can make that person know the Titan's name, even if they have never heard it before. {{Source|The Unseen Guide}} {{Source|Den vs Harrison}} This process can become more intense if it is the first time someone bonds with a Titan, like when [[Lok Lambert]] bonded with [[Kipperin]], which occasionally made him levitate and prevent falls by just having its [[Amulets|Amulet]] near him {{Source|A Seeker Is Born}}. When Lok bonded with [[Freelancer]], he was also shown brief glimpses of the future where he later uses the Titan. {{Source|The Casterwill Connection}}

Titans may unbond with their Seeker if the Seeker's personality changes dramatically.{{Sourceneeded}} A Titan will also unbond if its Seeker dies, although this may cause the Titan to go mad, as was the case with [[Madea]] or if their Amulet is lost for a long time  {{source|Ask Guggenheim}}. However, Titans such as [[Fenris]] and [[Antedeluvian]] have been known to stay invoked and protect the Seeker's secrets.  
[[File:S1E26 Professor Amulet of Will.jpg|thumb|[[The Professor]] loses control of [[Overlos]] as [[Archwarder]]'s ability targets the [[Amulet of Will]]]]
A spell known as [[Sorrowbond]] is capable of unnaturally severing the bond between Seeker and Titan and thus allowing another Seeker to attempt to bond with them. The affects of this spell, however, are overcome once the original Seeker touches the Titan's Amulet again. Titans themselves also have abilities that could interfere with the Seeker-Titan bond. [[Archwarder]], for example, can make powerful Titans hard to control or invoke; while [[Dendras]] can control enemy Titans, turning them against their Seekers and making them follow orders from Dendras' Seeker instead. [[Araknos]]' webs can also control the actions of other Titans, rendering them useless or making ally Titans fight each other. {{Source|The Secret of Two Generations}} [[Dominator]]'s spikes can make a Titan unpredictable and agitated, refusing to follow their Seeker's orders. {{Source|The Mission}}  [[Demigorgan]], the Legendary Titan of Betrayal, can also create Seeker and Titan mimics to subvert the bond and confuse both, hypnotizing them into thinking the mimics are real. {{Source|Dante's Return}} 

Titans can, in a way, remember past Seekers despite being currently bonded with another one. For example, [[Pendragon]] reacted to the Demigorgan mimic of the [[Champion of Casterwill|first Champion of Casterwill]] despite being currently [[Powerbond|powerbonded]] with [[Lok Lambert]]. {{Source|Dante's Return}}

[[File:S1E23 Tersly summons Venadek.jpg|thumb|180x180px|[[Tersly]] summons his signature Titan, [[Venadek]]]]
When a Titan is not invoked, it resides in the [[Spirit World]]. A Seeker can then invoke it by holding their Amulet and calling out their name, with a few exceptions. Usually, a Titan is summoned only after the Seeker has bonded with it, but this is not always the case, resulting in the summoned Titan simply not following the person's orders. {{Source|Memory Lane}} An exception to this is when [[Lord Casterwill]]'s spirit is present to help summon the Titan, in which case it will follow the Seeker's as if they were bonded. {{Source|Knight of the Willblade}}
[[File:S1E21 Dante collapasing Lok.png|thumb|[[Dante Vale]] collapses after invoking [[Caliban]], [[Metagolem]] and [[Behemoth]] within a few minutes]]
Summoning a Titan, and keeping it summoned, is a process that requires willpower, {{Source|The Casterwill Connection}} physical stamina and focus. As such, if one summons too many Titans, they make runout of willpower to summon any more or cast spells {{Source|The Divine Comedy}}. Similarly, they may also get physically tired and even pass out, depending on the strength of the Titans invoked and the interval between each summoning. {{Source|To Be Together}} {{Source|Coming of Age}} However, this exhaustion can be trained to be less significant over time. For example, the first time [[Zhalia Moon]] invoked [[King Basilisk]], she almost passed out {{Source|Absent Heroes}}, but later on she was able to invoke it as well as many other Titans without issue. {{Source|The Divine Comedy}} If a Seeker fails to concentrate when a Titan is invoked, it may transform into a glowing essence which will then return to its Amulet, such as when [[Den Fears]]' first Titan, [[Vigilante]], returned to its Amulet after Den was punched. {{Source|Den vs Harrison}} Something similar happened when [[Lok Lambert]] was hit by a [[Poisonfang]] spell, causing his [[Baselaird|Powerbonded Baselaird]] to fall to its knees. {{Source|The Tower of Nostradamus}}

A Titan will also return to its Amulet if it has taken too much physical damage, or if its Seekers orders it to do so. When a Titan is taken out the first way, its Seeker may feel discomfort and even pain, incapacitating them briefly and generally exhausting them; {{Source|Dante's Return}} the Titan will then be unable to be summoned again for an extended period of time, with only the spell [[Newlife]] being able to circumvent this. {{Source|The Divine Comedy}}{{Source|The Mission}} When a Titan is sent back to its Amulet the second way, however, it can be re-summoned at the Seeker's will. {{Source|The Spiral Mark}}{{Source|Rassimov's Secret}}

[[File:S1E26 Sophie Sabriel amulet dust.png|thumb|[[Sophie Casterwill]] cries as [[Sabriel]]'s Amulet fades into dust]]
While a Titan's spirit is usually immortal and returns safely to its Amulet when a standard battle is lost, there are rare instances where a Titan is permanently lost to its Seeker and its physical form is [[:Category:Destroyed Titans|destroyed]]. Titans can be entirely erased from existence through the use of dark magic or have their physical form permanently lost due to the destruction of their Amulet. In some cases, the spiritual energy of a Titan lives on and is permanently returned to the Spirit World, while in other cases, the Titan ceases to exist entirely. The destruction of an Amulet also causes great discomfort to the Titan's Seeker. {{Source|The Blood Spiral}} {{Source|S2E21}}

There are several unique ways for a Titan's physical form or Amulet to be destroyed, rendering them unable to be invoked:

*If a Seeker demands that a Titan does not return to its Amulet after taking too much damage, sustaining further consecutive blows will cause its Amulet to permanently disintegrate, destroying the Titan. {{Source|Dante's Return}} Similarly, if a Titan refuses to follow orders or cannot return due to external forces —such as the mental hijacking effect of [[Dominator]]'s spikes— it and its Amulet will be permanently destroyed after taking too many blows. {{Source|The Mission}} 

*The spell [[Darkvoid]] acts as a black hole that can completely disintegrate anything beyond its event horizon in seconds. In the case of a Titan, it can be pulled in and erased, with their Amulets destroyed for good. {{Source|Memory Lane}} 

*[[Legion|Legion, the Legendary Titan of War]], can absorb individual Titans and cause their physical Amulets to disintegrate. When Legion's  Amulet was later shattered by Lok Lambert using the [[Willblade]], Legion was destroyed, and the spirits of all the Titans it had absorbed over the centuries were freed and permanently sent back to the Spirit World. {{Source|The Spiral Mark}}

*[[Umbra]] represents a unique case of a Titan that suffers an unrecoverable drain on its energy whenever it teleports other living entities. {{Source|The Power of Umbra}} Pushing this process past its definitive limit causes the Titan's spirit to permanently disintegrate away. {{Source|Dante's Return}}

A Titan can also be permanently banished directly to the world of [[Spirit World|Huntik]] by the power of [[Overlos]], the Titan of Immortality, who can create an open portal that links the human dimension to the native spirit realm, and can send Titans into it leaving their physical anchors behind. {{Source|The Mission}}

In terms of appearance, Titans present in variety of forms, some of which can look like humans and certain animals. Generally, Titans do not talk, but do have the ability to make sounds such as grunting, groaning, screaming or other sounds<ref name=":0">Titans make these noises in the English dub. This can change in other dubs.</ref> depending for example on what animal they appear to relate to. They communicate with their Seekers telepathically. However, Titans are able to understand and follow their Seeker's orders. This communication becomes enhanced when a Titan becomes [[Powerbond|powerbonded]], also allowing Seekers to communicate telepathically. 
[[File:S2E47 Dante Ariel 3.png|thumb|[[Ariel|Powerbonded Ariel]] gestures at [[Dante Vale|Dante]] to back out|180x180px]]
Titans also do not think the way humans do, but rather behave like animals in many ways and will follow their instincts and do as their Seekers orders. For example, [[Gareon]] regularly comforts its Seeker, [[Zhalia Moon]], in a cat-like manner when she's feeling down. [[Cherit]] and [[Madea]] are notable known exceptions that can talk and even reason, the former can even play chess and acts nigh-identically to a human apart from the lack of physiological needs. Apart from these exceptions, some Titans are shown to display some level of critical thinking, such as when [[Ariel]] signaled [[Dante Vale]] to retreat after the ambush against [[Rassimov]] went south, or when [[Solwing]] tried to get Dante of the aforementioned situation by snatching the [[Necklace of Tutankhamun]] out of his hands and using it as bait for Rassimov. {{Source|Rassimov's Secret}}

Some Titans do appear to have what resembles a personality or temperament; [[Lindorm]], for example, requires its Seeker to be assertive and in control of their emotions, otherwise it will act aggressively and ignore orders. They're also shown to immediately feel threatened and distressed in the presence of [[Nullifiers]], such as when [[Lilith the Queen]] and [[Caliban|Powerbonded Caliban]] immediately stopped fighting each other when [[Void]] was invoked, looking to fight it instead. {{Source|The Spiral Mark}} In contrast to this, Titans will immediately stop what they are doing to make a bow in the presence of [[Lord Casterwill]]'s spirit. {{Source|Knight of the Willblade}} Titans generally lack any physiological needs that biological living beings do, such as the need for food, sleep or oxygen (though [[Cherit]] still shows enjoyment in eating). {{Source|The Casterwill Client}} {{Source|The Treasures of the Argonauts}} {{Source|Back Home}}
